  "toyota_summary": "The U.S. and Canada are negotiating a limited trade deal ahead of an August 19 deadline, with Section 338 tariffs under discussion. A partial agreement could reshape how the two countries handle cross-border auto supply chains, lowering some duties but likely keeping targeted levies in place. This matters because automakers source parts across North America, and any tariff carve-outs or new restrictions change sourcing math and production costs overnight.\n\nOil flowing through the Strait of Hormuz—about 5 million barrels daily even under Iran sanctions—is keeping Brent crude prices lower than they would otherwise be. Cheaper fuel reduces consumer incentive to buy electric vehicles and shifts demand back toward larger, higher-margin vehicles. Watch whether geopolitical flare-ups in the Middle East disrupt that flow.",
